FAIL SUMMARY - two defects against the written contract, plus one minor letter deviation (the lead may grade F3)

F1 (step 7, "the secret set ... passed between processes only via environment or pipes"). The harness passes the
   auth secrets on a COMMAND LINE. swarm/codex-check.sh:43
       pys() { env CD_SECRETS="$SECRETS_JSON" PYTHONUTF8=1 python3 "$CODEX_DIR/tools.py" "$@"; }
   runs /usr/bin/env with argv[1] = CD_SECRETS=[<every auth secret>]; env then execs python3. That hop is argv, not
   environment, and argv is world-readable (/proc/<pid>/cmdline is mode 0444 here; /proc is mounted without hidepid).
   Evidence:
   - strace of a real harness run (no shims but a docker CLI stub, Docker-free path):
       `strace -f -e trace=execve -s 400 bash swarm/codex-check.sh T 1` ->
       execve("/usr/bin/env", ["env", "CD_SECRETS=[\"rt_CTEST_REFRESH_...\", \"tok_CTEST_SECRET_...\"]", "PYTHONUTF8=1",
       "python3", ".../tools.py", "copy", ...
   - a full Docker run traced the same way ($D/trace.out): the secret list is on /usr/bin/env's argv three times per run
     (copy, verdict, keep).
   - an unprivileged /proc poller (a separate process that only opens /proc/<pid>/cmdline) CAUGHT it in 1 of 6
     Docker-free runs: "CAUGHT pid 3050881: env CD_SECRETS=["rt_CTEST_REFRESH_...", "tok_CTEST_SECRET_..."] PYTHONUTF8=1
     python3 ..." - i.e. any local user can read the user's Codex access and refresh tokens while the harness runs.
     (No auditd/atop/acct is active on this host, so nothing reached disk here; on a host with execve auditing it would.)
   - Remedy verified (rule 6b): with `pys() { CD_SECRETS="$SECRETS_JSON" PYTHONUTF8=1 python3 ...; }` (a bash prefix
     assignment: environment only) the same strace shows 0 execve lines carrying the secret and the copy step still runs.
   Neither the oracle nor the worker's suite looks at argv, so both pass with this defect.

F2 (DETAIL rule: "optionally followed by ONE tree-relative path ... A DETAIL NEVER contains ... host absolute paths
   ($CODEX_AUTH, ...)"). A manifest line with no sidecar line is echoed verbatim; tools.py safe_path() only applies the
   character regex, which admits a leading "/" and "..", and the `noline` check runs BEFORE the `badpath` check.
   Repro (end to end, the real harness; only `docker info` stubbed so step 1 passes - the copy step never touches docker):
     fixture manifest gets an extra line "<$HOME>/.codex/auth.json" (no sidecar line) ->
     REASON: fingerprint-mismatch
     DETAIL: a manifest path has no fingerprint line in the sidecar: <$HOME>/.codex/auth.json
   Same with a temp-dir path (DETAIL carried the full /tmp/claude-1000/... scratch path) and with "../../../../etc/shadow"
   (tools.py copy -> skip|fingerprint-mismatch|noline|../../../../etc/shadow). With a sidecar line the same paths give
   `badpath` and no path (correct). The input is a worker-written manifest, not Codex - but the rule is absolute and the
   skip record is an archive candidate in a public repo. Fix: refuse absolute/`..` paths in safe_path (or check badpath
   first). Not covered by the oracle or the worker's suite.

F3 (minor; step 6 "no last.json - meaning NO directory entry of that name at all"). If Codex writes a valid last.json
   and then `chmod 000 /out`, the entry exists but the harness's `[[ -e "$LAST" || -L "$LAST" ]]` cannot see through the
   unsearchable dir: have_last=0 -> classification -> skip `codex-error` ("Codex exited with an error or without an
   answer") instead of treating last.json as present (step 7 -> schema-invalid). $D/scen.outlock.log: REASON codex-error,
   dropped.txt "out<TAB>the private output dir could not be listed", audit 0 secret hits, temp dir removed. Consequence is
